+### Wave Scheduling
+
+- The plan's Dependency Map defines the waves: a task is ready when every
+  task it depends on is merged. Dispatch ALL ready tasks in one batch of
+  isolated implementer subagents — never serialize independent tasks.
+  Isolated subagents cannot conflict while running.
+- Each implementer's changes come back parked on a task ref; your worktree
+  stays clean while implementers run. Merging refs is governed by the
+  always-applied `isolated-task-merge` rule.
+- Review pre-merge: generate the review package from the parked ref (see
+  Handling Implementer Status) and only cherry-pick an approved ref.
+- Verdicts: approved → merge, mark complete, dispatch newly-ready tasks.
+  Fixable Critical/Important findings → merge, dispatch a fix subagent on
+  the merged state, review the fix's own ref. Fundamentally wrong → delete
+  the ref unmerged, re-dispatch the implementer with the findings.
+- When a wave is fully merged, dispatch a wave review over the whole wave
+  diff (`scripts/review-package <wave-base> HEAD`; wave-base = HEAD before
+  the wave's first merge) before starting the next wave.
